hello everybody this is Peter Cooper  from the NCBI service desk and welcome  to another NCBI webinar this is one of  our shorter ones the ones we call the  NCBI minutes so we'll be talking for  about ten minutes here to some reminders  and that's what this slide is for that  the recording of this webinar will be  available on our YouTube channel let me  take us about a week to ten days to get  that up there the materials which  basically are some backup slides between  kind of screenshots we're going to show  you today are available on our FTP site  and compressed URL there will take you  to those materials so today Melissa  landrum who is the lead developer for  the clinvar resources is going to be  talking about the new clinvar submission  wizard and I'm going to turn it over to  Melissa right now thanks Peter hello  everyone thanks for joining us today  clinvar is the ncbi database for  archiving interpretations of variants  relative to diseases and other  phenotypes all interpretations in  clinvar are provided by our submitters  namely clinical testing labs research  labs resources like mm Jane reviews and  locus-specific databases as well as  expert panels and practice guidelines a  link to information on how to submit to  clinvar is available from the clinvar  homepage submitters who have several  interpretations to submit or submitters  that submit repeatedly like clinical  testing labs use our submission  spreadsheet templates and there's a  section here on this page with  information about those templates for  researchers and other submitters who  submit a single variant at a time it may  be easier to use the clinvar submission  wizard to guide you through the  submission process for example a genetic  counselor may review a variant and a lab  report and want to submit her own  interpretation to clinvar or a research  lab may be publishing a paper on a new  causative variant for disease to get to  the wizard from this page you can click  on how to submit and you'll see a link  here to ncbi's variation submission  portal so we'll click on that also note  that you can get to the submission  portal from ncbi's home page for this  demo today I'm  going to switch to an internal site but  note that when you use the submission  portal you will not need to make any  changes to the URL when you first login  to the variation submission portal  you'll need to register your  organization you can use this help link  at the top of the page if you need more  information to do that once your  organization is registered and approved  on this page you'll see the name of your  organization so here from my demo my  organization name is clinvar submission  test lab and that will be followed by  two buttons one is for the clinvar  single submission wizard and the second  is to upload a new file submission like  a spreadsheet so today we're going to  click on the button for the submission  wizard the wizard walks you through a  series of tabs for different kinds of  information the first tab is for general  information about your submission here  you'll provide a name for the submission  which is just for your own use in  submission portal you can use the name  that's provided or you can give your own  name so today I'm going to submit a  variant that causes CFC syndrome so I'm  going to name my submission CFC one  variant one if your submission is part  of a named study like the caesar project  or the Framingham study you can provide  the name and a description of that study  in these boxes if your submission is  from a standalone research project you  can just leave these boxes blank you can  let us know if you need your submission  held until the corresponding paper is  published by selecting hold until  published here for this example I'm  going to select public so that my  submission is not held if the citation  is already available you can provide  that information in this section so  today for my demo I'm going to provide a  publication using a PubMed ID note that  on each tab your work is not saved until  you click the save and continue' button  but that does mean that you can start a  submission save your work for one or  more tabs and come back to the form  later to complete the process so I'll  click  save and continue and that takes us to  the next tab where you can indicate  which organizations should get  attribution for the submission if your  submission is from a single organization  like a single research lab you can  select single organization here and save  immediately the organization for which  you have logged on will be used  automatically if your submission is from  multiple groups each of those groups  needs to be registered and approved in  the submission portal first and you will  need to have been defined as a submitter  for each organization then when you  select multiple organizations all of  those groups are available in this table  1 should be specified as the primary  organization for the clinvar submission  and any number of others can be  specified as additional organizations  for today's example I'm going to submit  from a single organization use the next  tab to define the variant that you've  interpreted the interpreted variant may  be a single variant like a single  nucleotide substitution to single  variants as a compound heterozygote or  multiple variants insists as a haplotype  to describe the location and of the  variant and the change that you observed  you can use either HCBS nomenclature or  chromosome coordinates here called  sequence location when you select the  type of variant and method of  description the page updates to provide  the appropriate fields for you to fill  in for this example I'm submitting a  single variant in the b-raf gene so I'm  going to describe with an HTTPS  expression so I will fill in the  reference sequence with accession and  version a description of the sequence  change so that would be say the C dot or  g dot portion of an HTTPS expression I'm  going to fill in the gene note that you  can start typing the gene symbol and  you'll get a list from which to choose  I'm also going to provide an alternate  designation here this is the protein  name but this could be any other name by  which that variant is known including  other nucleotide descriptions with  legacy numbering systems there are other  optional fields to fill in you could  provide a variation identifier like a DB  snip RS number you can tell us the  location of the variance or the intron  or X on that it's in your local  identifier for the variant if you have a  database or the functional consequence  note that most of these fields on this  tab are optional so you can just fill in  the information that you have variants  in clinvar are generally interpreted  with respect to a disease or condition  so this condition is indicated on the  conditions tab if you have information  to submit unobserved phenotypes or  clinical features please wait to submit  them on the observations tab the  condition for which you are interpreting  the variant can be described with a  database identifiers like an om number  or you can provide the name of the  disease in this box in some cases a  variant is submitted to describe its  effect on a person's response to a drug  so for these cases you can select drug  response here and the page updates to  let you enter the drug name and  optionally the condition for which that  drug is used for this example I'm going  to submit on a disease CFC 1 syndrome  and I'm going to do so with an ohm  number use this tab to describe your  interpretation of the clinical  significance of the variant you must  provide a value for the clinical  significance and the options for that  are available in this pulldown menu so  I'm going to submit that this variant is  likely pathogenic and then optionally  you can also provide other information  such as the mode of inheritance for that  variant again options are provided in a  menu you can provide the date that the  clinical significance of that variant  was last evaluated and you can also  provide a comment that describes why you  classified the variant as you did this  tab also lets you provide information  about the method that you use to  classify variants which in clinvar we  call your assertion criteria  more information about this is available  on the clinvar website using this link  but in short this is a document that  describes the categories that you use to  classify variants such as benign  uncertain and pathogenic and the  criteria that you require to assign a  variant to each category if this  information is in your publication  describing the variant the PubMed ID can  be provided as the assertion criteria  here we do encourage submission of  assertion criteria if it is available  because it affects the review status or  number of stars on the resulting clinvar  record but it is not required so you can  certainly submit your data to clinvar  without assertion criteria leaving this  section blank clinvar submissions also  include the evidence that supports the  interpretation in the form of  observations of the variant those  observations may be described as  aggregates individuals or experimental  results at least one observation is  required per submission if you want to  submit data for many individuals as a  group you can use the aggregate section  you can submit information about more  than one group as distinct aggregates  for example if you had information from  a case control study you could complete  a section for the cases and another for  the controls in other words all of the  information in this observation applies  to everyone in this group  if you prefer you can provide  information about each individual with  the variant so in this case all of the  information is specific to that person  such as that person's age their  ethnicity or the phenotypes that were  observed in that individual for research  groups who have performed functional  assays to evaluate a variant that  experimental evidence can also be  described that information might include  the cell line used in the experiment or  the tissue type that was analyzed and  you can also provide a comment that  describes the results of your experiment  more structured details about the  experimental methods can also be  provided for this example I'm going to  submit one aggregate observation so this  is information about a group of affected  individuals with this disease so I'm  going to name my observation going to  indicate yes that they were affected  with the disease I'm going to indicate  that the allele origin was germline this  variant was detected as a germline  variant and I'm going to indicate that  this data was collected with clinical  testing I'm also going to indicate that  this was seen in a mixed group of males  and females the age range for these  individuals is from 1 to 20 years and  I'm going to indicate that i saw this  variant in six families and a total of  six single heterozygotes when you've  entered your information make sure to  click Save and continue and when you've  entered all of your observations click  Save and continue which takes you to the  final tab this is an overview of the  information that you've provided you can  take this opportunity to review the data  and go back and correct anything if  necessary once you're finished click the  submit button at the bottom of the page  this is a critical step this sends your  data to clinvar and sends you an email  confirming that you've submitted your  data when we receive the submission one  of the clinvar curators will check it  and contact you if there are any  questions about the data  otherwise your data will be promptly  processed and made available on the  clinvar website we encourage you to try  the clinvar submission wizard and we  welcome your feedback you can use the  contact link at the top of this page to  send us any questions or suggestions  about the wizard so if we have time for  a few questions if anyone wants to send  those in we'd be happy to answer them so  the first question that we have is how  do i know if i should use the wizard or  use a spreadsheet to submit to clinvar  so our recommendation is if you're  submitting a very small number of  variants at a time let's say less than  five it will probably be useful to use  the wizard if you're submitting more  variants let's say more than five or ten  variants it you will probably get faster  processing of your submission if you use  the spreadsheet because that makes it in  a more efficient process for our curator  to review many variants at a time and  get back to you with any issues so that  you can be processed more quickly the  second question we have here is if I  find that I've made a mistake in my  submission can I get into edit it so if  you have already sent your submission  into clinvar so here in the table you  can track the progress of your  submission so this is the one I just  entered and you can see the status is  submitted and it does not have an edit  button here so once you've already sent  it to clinvar it is not available to  edit if one of our curators find  something that needs to be fixed we will  change the status so that there is an  edit button here available for you to  fix your data if you have not yet  submitted your your wizard submission  you will see an edit button here and you  can go in and make any edits until  you're ready to submit so we have  another question should people only use  the wizard for submissions where they  have made their own assertion on the  variant or would clinicians use this to  report assertions directly from lab  reports so you can certainly use it to  submit your own assertion of the variant  so whether or not clinicians can use it  to report directly from lab reports you  actually for that I would actually  suggest at this point that if you want  would like to do that you can use the  spreadsheet and the reason is that the  lab themselves may have already  submitted that interpretation to clinvar  and if you are going to submit somebody  else's assertion we need to know who the  testing laboratory was so that is a  feature that we will add to the wizard  in a future release but for now that  information can be submitted on the  spreadsheet so if you would like to  submit that kind of information and you  need help with the spreadsheet please do  feel free to contact us ok this is Peter  Cooper I'm back again thanks Melissa  that was great that's all we have time  for today so we'll have the slides  materials that sort of and show you  those screenshots available on our  webinar some courses page and we'll have  this recording up on our YouTube channel  probably within a week or ten days  thanks everybody for coming i'm going to  go ahead and the webinar  you

